window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t":"Introduction","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/einfuehrung/index.html","x":"CncCraft is a free, parametric CAD/CAM program for beginners: you design parts for a CNC router with dimensions you can change, compute the toolpaths and hand them to the machine as a file. Parametric means: a dimension is not a fixed line but a value you can change at any time – and everything tied to it follows. Write the board width as the variable breite , for example, and holes, distances, construction lines and toolpaths move along when you change breite . What professional CAD programs like Fusion 360 can do, CncCraft brings in a simple form – no licence, no subscription, no account. The path from idea to milled part has three stages in CncCraft, all in one window: Design: you draw shapes or type dimensions – numbers, variables or formulas. Layers, snapping, construction lines and constraints help you work precisely (start here: parametrisch-uebersicht). Generate toolpaths: you select shapes and say what the router should do with them – cut out, clear a pocket, mill a slot, drill or carve lettering with a V-bit. For the chosen material CncCraft suggests matching cutting values and warns when one is too high. Output: after a preview in 2D or 3D you save the toolpaths as G-code. The window at a glance Area What you find there Menu bar (top left) File, Edit, View, Toolpaths, Help. Layer picker (top centre) The active layer and the list of all layers. See ebenenliste. Toolbar (left) Drawing and editing tools, plus job setup and variables. Header above the drawing Decoration (origin, grid, snapping, dimensions, rulers) and constraints; the view buttons on the right. Drawing area (centre) The workpiece with rulers, construction lines and your shapes – or the 3D preview. Toolpaths (right) Board setup, the buttons for new toolpaths, the list, preview and output. Status line (bottom) Size of the selection, pointer position and the layer of the selection. More on the entwurfsansicht page. To dive straight in, open the “sign with lettering, pocket and holes” example on the start page and see what a finished job looks like. CncCraft is a standalone re-creation that follows Spark by Vectric and extends it in places. Only what CncCraft can do is documented here.","n":false},{"t":"Installation","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/installation/index.html","x":"CncCraft is built from source; the <code>build.sh</code> script produces the packages for macOS, Windows and Linux. All packages are produced with one call to build.sh (or just make ) in the program's folder. Finished files land in the dist/ subfolder. Command Result Runs on ./build.sh mac CncCraft.app , .zip and .dmg for Apple Silicon a Mac ./build.sh mac-intel the same for Intel Macs a Mac ./build.sh windows CncCraft.exe , .zip and – with Inno Setup – an installer Windows ./build.sh linux .tar.gz and .deb (x86_64) Linux ./build.sh all everything possible on this machine Requirements The Rust language with cargo (for compiling). macOS 12 or newer for the Mac packages. For the Windows installer: Inno Setup. Building packages on GitHub If you have no Windows or Linux machines of your own, you can have all packages built on GitHub: the included workflow .github/workflows/release.yml starts as soon as a version tag like v0.1.0 is pushed, and attaches the packages to the release. The Mac app is only “ad hoc” signed. On other Macs a security warning appears on first launch; without an Apple developer account it cannot be avoided. The Windows and Linux routes are described but have not yet been tried on every system. Checking that it runs With cnccraft --selftest the program runs a self-test and reports every step. With cnccraft --screenshot image.png it saves a picture of the window without any input from you – that is how the pictures in this help were made.","n":false},{"t":"Start page","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/startseite/index.html","x":"At launch CncCraft shows three tiles: new file, open file and the example. The start page appears as long as no job is open yet. You can reach it later at any time via File ▸ Start page . Tile What happens Create new file Opens the neuer-job dialog, where you set the material size and thickness. Open file Loads a saved project (extension .cnccraft ). See dateien-speichern. Open example Loads a sign with lettering, a pocket and holes, with toolpaths – a good starting point to try the preview. The example is also available at any time via File ▸ Open example .","n":false},{"t":"Create and set up a new job","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/neuer-job/index.html","x":"The job describes the workpiece: size, material and the router's origin. The dialog is called “Create job” when you begin a new file, and “Set up job” when you open it later via Edit ▸ Set up job … , the button in the toolbar or the “Board setup” row on the right. Job size Width (X), length (Y), thickness (Z): the dimensions of the blank. The small sketch shows which side matches which axis. Units: inches or millimetres. On switching, the numbers are converted; internally the file is stored in millimetres. Material No job can be created without a material – the Create button stays grey until one is chosen. The list holds ten materials from softwood to aluminium, each with a small swatch. It determines the tools and cutting values (see werkzeugauswahl), the warnings for cuts that are too fast or too deep, and the look in the 3D view. Recommendations (collapsed): a click shows spindle speed, maximum feed, stepdown, suitable cutters and cooling for the material. Manage … at the end of the list opens the material settings, where you adjust values and add your own materials (materialien). Origin Z zero: Surface – the top of the material is height 0, cut depths count downward. Table – the bottom is height 0. XY zero: Corner (lower left) or Centre of the workpiece. That is where the origin sits in the G-code file later too. Safe Z and Start Z: the heights the router travels at between cuts, and the machine's start height. Size and thickness are then available as the variables platte_b , platte_l and platte_d . So a cut depth can be entered as platte_d/2 , for example – see variablen. If you change an existing job, CncCraft recomputes all toolpaths with the new values. A new job always starts with your last settings – size, units, material and origin. If you mostly mill the same board, you only have to click Create .","n":false},{"t":"The design view","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/entwurfsansicht/index.html","x":"In the 2D view you draw and edit; rulers, grid and snapping help you. The drawing area shows the workpiece from above: the board with rulers along the top and left edge and the coordinate cross at the origin. The value in the ruler is in the job's unit. A click in the ruler adds a construction line. Moving around Zoom: with the mouse wheel or a two-finger gesture (toward the pointer). Pan: drag with the middle or right mouse button. Whole view ( F4 ): shows the whole workpiece. Zoom to selection: shows the selected shapes. The header Above the drawing are two groups of switches: Decoration: show the origin, snap to the grid, snap to geometry (see einrasten), show the dimensions, and show or hide the rulers with the construction lines. Each decoration is also a tool: the grid is only visible while snapping to it is on; the rulers are only there while you can set construction lines with them. Constraints: Distance ( D ) and Level/upright ( H ) – see abstand-bemassen and waagrecht-senkrecht. Top right, the 2D/3D button switches between design and the 3D view; next to it a button turns the origin (axis cross) on or off. The toolbar On the left the tools sit in groups: mode (select, transform, node edit), lines, shapes, text, edit objects, align and mirror, offset, pattern, boolean operations and – set apart – job setup and the variables. The small arrow at the top left shrinks the bar; each group then shows only the last tool used. Hover over a tool and a short tooltip appears. The status line at the bottom also says what to do next (for example “Pick the second point”). Worksheet decorations What the drawing area shows in addition you switch one by one in View ▸ Worksheet : board, rulers, coordinate cross and hints. The grid is not there – it follows the “Snap to grid” switch. Colours Shapes, their toolpaths in the list and the drawn cutter paths all carry the colour of their layer. Change a layer's colour (ebenenliste) and all three change with it.","n":false},{"t":"The 3D view","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/ansicht-3d/index.html","x":"The 3D view shows the workpiece as it will look after milling. With the 2D/3D button top right (or F2 for 2D and F3 for 3D) you switch the view. In 3D you see the material as a solid; what the toolpaths remove is cut away. Controls Rotate: drag with the left mouse button. Pan: drag with the middle or right mouse button. Zoom: mouse wheel or two-finger gesture. Perspective: in View ▸ Perspective (3D) you switch between perspective and parallel projection. Orientation aids Two decorations help you get your bearings in 3D; both can be switched off individually in View ▸ 3D view : View cube: shows which side you are looking from. Origin axis cross: draws the router's axis cross (X red, Y green, Z blue) at the job's origin. It follows the chosen origin (corner or centre, surface or table). The origin button shows or hides the axis cross in both views at once. The toolpaths themselves you overlay on the workpiece as lines with the “Show G-code motion in 3D” option; more under vorschau-werkzeugwege.","n":true},{"t":"Layers","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/ebenenliste/index.html","x":"Layers group shapes: they can be shown and hidden, locked and coloured. Top centre, the layer picker shows which layer you are drawing on. A click opens the list of all layers. Element Meaning Eye Visible / hidden. Hidden shapes are not shown, not milled and not exported. Lock Locked / unlocked. Locked shapes stay visible but cannot be clicked or changed. Name A click makes the layer active – new shapes are created on it. Double-click renames it. Colour dot The layer's colour; a click changes it. At the bottom of the list + adds a new layer (ebene-neu), − deletes the active layer (ebene-loeschen). Selection to here moves the selected shapes onto the active layer. A drawing always has at least one layer; the last one cannot be deleted.","n":false},{"t":"Add a new layer","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/ebene-neu/index.html","x":"A new layer is created with a click on the plus in the layer list. Open the layer list (click the layer picker, top centre). Click + at the bottom. The new layer gets a name and a colour from the palette and becomes active at once. Give it your own name by double-click if you like. To put shapes you have already drawn onto the new layer, select them and click Selection to here . One layer per work step is handy, for example “outline”, “lettering” and “holes”. With the eye you then switch off parts of the drawing on purpose, for example to preview the lettering only.","n":false},{"t":"Delete a layer","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/ebene-loeschen/index.html","x":"When deleting a layer you decide what happens to its shapes. Make the layer active with a click. Click − in the layer list. If the layer holds shapes, CncCraft asks (“Layer … holds n shape(s)”): you can move the shapes to another layer or delete them too. The last remaining layer cannot be deleted. Deleting can be undone with ⌘Z (Windows/Linux: Ctrl+Z ).","n":false},{"t":"Theme: light, dark or like the system","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/einstellungen-design/index.html","x":"CncCraft can appear light or dark, or follow your operating system's theme. In View ▸ Theme you choose between System , Light and Dark . Top right in the title bar a button (moon or sun) switches between light and dark. The choice is saved and used again next time. The drawing sheet stays light in both themes, so the colours of shapes and toolpaths look the same. Text, input fields and buttons are easy to read in both themes – an automatic test checks the contrast ratio of every surface.","n":false},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/tastenkuerzel/index.html","x":"The most important commands have keys. The “Keyboard shortcuts” list in the Help menu shows them in the program. On the Mac ⌘ (Command) applies, on Windows and Linux Ctrl . Help ▸ Keyboard shortcuts ( ⌘/ ) opens the list in the program; the same keys also stand next to the menu entries. Tools Key Action S Select L Draw line P Draw spline R Draw rectangle C Draw circle O Offset vectors T Trim vectors at crossings N Edit selected vector in node mode / back D Dimension distance H Set level/upright While drawing a line Enter Finish line Tab Close line Space Finish line and start a new one Esc Cancel Edit and file ⌘Z / ⇧⌘Z Undo / redo ⌘C , ⌘X , ⌘V Copy, cut, paste ⌘D Duplicate ⌘G / ⇧⌘G Group / ungroup ⌘A Select all Del / ⌫ Delete ⌘N , ⌘O New job, open ⌘S / ⇧⌘S Save / save as View F2 / F3 2D view / 3D view F4 Whole view Mouse and keys together ⇧ + click select and deselect several objects ⇧ while dragging snapping off ⌥ or ⌃ while moving make a copy Arrow keys move the selection or nodes ( ⇧ : ten times) ↑ ↓ in dimension fields switch between the fields Formula and = evaluate the value Tab in dimension fields accept the suggested variable Long-press a value type a dimension, radius or construction-line distance The single keys for tools ( S , L , P , R , C , O , T , D , H ) follow programs like Fusion 360, so drawing needs no trip to the toolbar.","n":true},{"t":"Saving and opening files","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/dateien-speichern/index.html","x":"Projects are saved as <code>.cnccraft</code> files and hold the drawing, layers, variables, constraints and toolpaths. Command Effect File ▸ New … Begins a new job (neuer-job). File ▸ Open … Loads a project ( .cnccraft ). File ▸ Open recent The projects opened and saved most recently, newest at the top – as in word processors. Clear list at the end tidies them up. Until something has been opened, the entry is grey. File ▸ Open example Loads the included example. File ▸ Save / Save as … Writes the project to a file. File ▸ Save toolpaths … Writes the machine file (G-code), see werkzeugwege-speichern. Unsaved changes If the drawing has changes that are not saved yet, a dot (●) appears after the file name at the top right. If you then want to close the program, begin a new job, or open another file or the example, CncCraft first asks “Do you want to save the changes …?”: Save: saves (for a new file CncCraft asks for the name) and then continues. Cancel the save and the question stays open. Don't save: continues without saving; the changes are lost. Cancel (or Esc ): nothing happens, you keep working. The construction lines are saved with the project too, with their formulas, attached shapes and lock. The project is a text file in JSON format; older files with the earlier extension .funke can still be opened.","n":true},{"t":"Undo and redo","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/rueckgaengig-wiederholen/index.html","x":"Every step can be taken back and redone. ⌘Z takes back the last step, ⇧⌘Z redoes it (also in the Edit menu). Up to 100 steps are remembered. Not only changes to shapes can be taken back, but every action one by one: drawing, moving, changing and deleting shapes, layers: add, duplicate, rename, reorder, show and hide, lock, change colour and opacity, merge, flatten and delete, toolpaths: reorder and delete, construction lines: add, move, change their distance and delete. A drag with the mouse is always one step – even if a shape or construction line was moved for a long time.","n":false},{"t":"Scripts and automation","c":"Getting started","u":"Help/page/skript-automatisierung/index.html","x":"A small script drives CncCraft step by step – the visible mouse shows every click. This is a pilot feature (beta). Scripting and the built-in automation server are new, still being refined and may change. They are meant mainly for recording demos; everyday work needs none of it. CncCraft can run a script : a plain list of commands, one per line, that it carries out by moving the visible mouse and clicking the real buttons – so it looks exactly like someone using the program. This is ideal for recording short demo videos. The script window Open it with Help ▸ Script… . It is its own window you can move anywhere. It has an editor with autocomplete and syntax highlighting, a Run and a Stop button, a Command help window listing every command, and an agent.md button that copies a full guide for an AI assistant. How a script reads One command per line, for example draw_rectangle cx=50 cy=50 width=40 height=30 . Write name = tool … to keep the result under a name, then use it later as $name.id – so you can draw a shape and give it a toolpath. Lines like move_to , click , drag and type_text play through the visible cursor; screenshot , recording_start / recording_stop and window width=… height=… help with recording. text_title and text_subtitle put a caption on screen, replay_speed sets how fast the mouse moves. Snapping from a script Every button has a command, so a script can switch the three snap methods (Snapping) on and off like any other: snap_to_geometry (bounding box), snap_to_center and snap_to_gap . That way a demo can show, step by step, how a shape lines up on an edge, a centre or an equal gap. Automation server Behind the scenes the same commands are an automation server running inside CncCraft on your own computer only (localhost). An AI assistant can connect to it and build a drawing for you while the visible mouse shows every move. Nothing leaves your machine.","n":true},{"t":"Select and move","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/auswahlwerkzeug/index.html","x":"With the select tool you click shapes, move them and change their size. The select tool (arrow, key S ) is CncCraft's normal mode. Selecting Click a shape to select it. A click on empty space clears the selection. Drag a box to select all shapes inside it. ⇧ + click adds shapes to the selection or takes them out again. ⌘A selects everything. The selection appears with a box, handles at the corners and a centre mark. The status line at the bottom shows the width and height of the selection. Moving and scaling Move: drag the shape – or the centre mark. With ⌥ or ⌃ while dragging a copy is made; with ⇧ nothing snaps. Arrow keys move the selection in small steps, with ⇧ ten times. Resize: drag a handle. ⇧ keeps the aspect ratio. Typing dimensions Enter with exactly one shape selected opens the dimension fields right at the shape ( Edit ▸ Edit dimensions ). With ↑ ↓ or Tab you switch fields. See eigenschaften-masse. Help while moving While dragging, the selection snaps to nodes, centres, midpoints, job corners and the grid – more under einrasten.","n":false},{"t":"Transform objects","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/transformieren/index.html","x":"The “Transform objects” form sets a shape's position and size by numbers. The “Transform objects” button in the toolbar ( Mode group) opens a form for the selected shape to the left of the drawing. It is the same block of fields as under eigenschaften-masse: Centre X / Centre Y – where the shape's centre lies (from the job's origin). Width / Height for rectangle and ellipse, Radius for circle and polygon, Corners for the polygon, Corner radius for the rectangle. Text and Height for text. Every field takes numbers, formulas and variables (formeln-in-feldern). Change a value and the shape follows at once; a click on the cross closes the form. If several shapes are selected, the form shows only their count – to move and scale them together use the select tool.","n":false},{"t":"Draw lines and polylines","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/linie-polylinie/index.html","x":"The line tool makes open or closed polylines from straight segments. Choose the line tool (key L ) and click the corner points into the drawing one after another. Between clicks a preview shows where the next segment leads. Snapping (einrasten) helps you hit points exactly. Key Action Enter or double-click finish the polyline (open) Tab close the polyline: the last point joins the first Space finish and start a new polyline at once Esc cancel Only closed polylines can be cut out or cleared. Open lines suit “on the line” profiles and V-carving. A single segment can later be set to a fixed length with Distance (abstand-bemassen) or made level or upright with Level/upright (waagrecht-senkrecht).","n":false},{"t":"Draw a rectangle","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/rechteck/index.html","x":"Draw rectangles from one corner to the opposite one; rounded corners are a dimension field. Choose the rectangle (key R ), press at a corner and drag to the opposite one. On release the rectangle is finished and selected. Under eigenschaften-masse you set the centre, width and height exactly; the corner radius rounds the corners. The corner radius also affects the toolpaths.","n":false},{"t":"Draw a circle","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/kreis/index.html","x":"Draw circles from the centre; the radius is a dimension field. Choose the circle (key C ), press on the centre and drag to the radius you want. The centre and radius can then be typed exactly under eigenschaften-masse. Circles are the basis for holes: the drilling toolpath drills in the centre of every selected contour.","n":false},{"t":"Draw an ellipse","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/ellipse/index.html","x":"An ellipse has two dimensions: width and height. Choose the ellipse and drag the bounding box it should fit into. Width and height can then be set more precisely under eigenschaften-masse. An ellipse with equal width and height is a circle.","n":false},{"t":"Draw a polygon","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/polygon/index.html","x":"A polygon is a regular n-gon with an adjustable number of corners. Choose the polygon, press on the centre and drag to the size you want. It has at least three corners; change the number of corners and the radius under eigenschaften-masse. The example contains a hexagon.","n":false},{"t":"Create text","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/text-erstellen/index.html","x":"Text is inserted as a vector shape and can then be milled or converted to curves. Choose the text tool and click in the drawing. A sample text is inserted; change its content in the Text field under eigenschaften-masse, and the size with the Height field. Lettering suits V-carving especially: the V-bit runs the letters at different depths by width. To edit the individual letter outlines, first convert the text to curves.","n":false},{"t":"Node editing","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/knotenbearbeitung/index.html","x":"In node mode you drag a shape's corner points one by one, insert them or delete them. Select a shape and press N (or choose the “Node editing” tool). The shape now shows its nodes. Drag a node: with the mouse. While dragging the node snaps to help points; ⇧ turns that off. Insert a node: double-click the line. Delete a node: select the node and press Del . Move with the arrow keys: selected nodes in small steps ( ⇧ : ten times). N or Esc ends node mode. With no shape selected the status line reports “Select a vector first, then press ‘N’”. Circles, rectangles and other basic shapes become free curves when node-edited. Dimensions that were tied to variables fall away – the shape then has no reference sizes any more.","n":false},{"t":"Offset","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/versatz/index.html","x":"Offset creates a contour at a fixed distance – larger or smaller than the original. Select one or more shapes and open the “Offset vectors” tool (key O ). In the form on the left you set: Distance in millimetres, Outside (the new contour is larger) or Inside (smaller). The button in the form creates the new contour as an extra shape and selects it; the original stays. If the offset gives no shape – for example because the contour vanishes entirely inward – the status line reports it (“Offset gives no shape”). A typical use: a border at a fixed distance from the board, or a contour that should be larger by the cutter radius.","n":false},{"t":"Mirror","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/spiegeln/index.html","x":"Mirror flips the selected shapes about their centre, level or upright. Select the shapes and in the “Align and mirror” group click Mirror horizontally or Mirror vertically . The shapes are flipped about the centre of the selection; with several shapes their arrangement to one another is kept.","n":false},{"t":"Centre on the material","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/zentrieren/index.html","x":"Centring moves the selection to the middle of the workpiece. Select the shapes and click Centre on the material (“Align and mirror” group). The centre of the whole selection is placed on the centre of the board; its size does not change.","n":false},{"t":"Convert to curves","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/in-kurven-umwandeln/index.html","x":"Converting turns basic shapes and text into free curves whose nodes you can edit. Select shapes or text and click Convert to curves (“Edit objects” group). A circle becomes a curve of nodes, text becomes the outlines of the letters. After that everything can be shaped in node editing mode. Converted shapes no longer have dimension fields like “radius” or “text”, and variables on them are released. So if you still want to change the shape parametrically, convert it last.","n":false},{"t":"Boolean operations","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/boolesche-operationen/index.html","x":"Union, subtract and intersect build a new shape from several closed shapes. Select at least two closed shapes and in the “Boolean operations” group click one of the three buttons: Operation Result Union One shape covering all selected areas. Subtract The shape selected first, with all others subtracted from it. Intersect Only the area all selected shapes have in common. The original shapes are replaced by the result. With ⌘Z you undo the step. For “Subtract” the order matters: select the base shape first (the board, for example), then the cut-outs with ⇧ + click.","n":false},{"t":"Copy, paste and duplicate","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/kopieren-einfuegen/index.html","x":"Shapes can be copied and pasted – with their toolpaths and the constraints between them. Command Key Effect Copy ⌘C puts the selection on the clipboard Cut ⌘X copies and deletes Paste ⌘V pastes the copy Duplicate ⌘D copies and pastes in one step Delete Del removes the selection All commands are also in the Edit menu. On Windows and Linux Ctrl applies instead of ⌘ . When copying, CncCraft takes along what hangs on the shapes: Toolpaths: if a shape belongs to a toolpath, a matching toolpath is made for the copy when pasting. Constraints: distance and level/upright constraints are copied only if all shapes they connect are part of the copy. Hold ⌥ or ⌃ while moving and a copy is made at the destination.","n":true},{"t":"Snapping","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/einrasten/index.html","x":"While drawing and moving, the pointer snaps to help points so everything sits exactly. While moving a shape, CncCraft lines it up on the other shapes and groups with three snap methods. Each has its own button in the header, in the Snapping group, and you turn them on and off on their own or all together: Snap to geometry (bounding box) – the moved shape's bounding box lines up with the edges, corners and nodes of other shapes and whole groups. X and Y line up on their own, so a shape far to the side can still sit at the same height as another. A dashed line through the caught edge shows the alignment. Snap to centre – the centre of the shape lines up on the centre of another shape or group. Snap to equal gap – the shape takes the same gap to a neighbour as another pair of shapes has between them (equal spacing). The matching distance is drawn as a labelled arrow, both for the gap you have chosen and for the reference gap it matched. Only the elements and groups on the sheet are snap anchors – the workpiece itself is not. While drawing With a drawing tool the pointer snaps to the corners and centres of existing shapes and, separately, to the grid . A small marker and a hint beside the pointer name what it caught. Settings The three snap buttons sit in the Snapping group; Snap to grid is in the Decoration group. Hold ⇧ while dragging and nothing snaps. The grid snaps with its spacing in millimetres and the angle step while drawing a line goes from 0° up to 90°, exactly as in the original program. Pilot feature. The three snap methods and their guide lines are new in CncCraft and still being refined; please report anything that looks off. The grid is only visible while Snap to grid is on – what you see is what snaps. In addition, moved shapes snap to construction lines: with an edge or their centre, and the line colours the shape red while it would snap.","n":false},{"t":"Dimensions and properties","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/eigenschaften-masse/index.html","x":"Every shape has dimension fields where you type numbers, formulas or variables. The fields appear in the Transform objects form and – with a shape selected and Enter pressed – right at the shape. Shape Fields Rectangle Centre X, Centre Y, Width, Height, Corner radius Circle Centre X, Centre Y, Radius Ellipse Centre X, Centre Y, Width, Height Polygon Centre X, Centre Y, Radius, Corners Text Centre X, Centre Y, Text, Height Curve (polyline) Number of points, closed or open, contours Entry The decimal mark is the comma or the point. Tab , ↑ and ↓ switch between fields; Enter accepts, Esc cancels. The fields do maths: 300/2 - 12 or breite/2 is a valid entry (formeln-in-feldern). Values must be greater than zero where a dimension requires it (a radius, for example).","n":false},{"t":"Draw a spline","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/spline/index.html","x":"A smooth curve through the points you click. The Spline tool (key P ) lays a smooth curve through every point you click. A double-click or Enter finishes it; a click on the first point closes it into a closed shape that can be cleared as a pocket, for example. The points can then be moved with node editing; the curve stays smooth.","n":true},{"t":"Trim, delete and join vectors","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/vektoren-trennen-verbinden/index.html","x":"Split vectors at crossings, remove individual pieces and join open ends. Tool How it works Trim ( T ) Click a vector: it is split at every crossing with other vectors. Delete vector Click a vector or a trimmed piece: it is removed. So you can take away overhanging ends after trimming. Join Click two points one after another: they are joined with a line. Click both ends of a polyline and it closes. You need closed vectors for pockets and cut-outs with an inside and outside; open lines suit profiles and slots.","n":false},{"t":"Round corners and ears","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/ecken-abrunden/index.html","x":"Round corners with a radius or cut an “ear” so parts fit into one another. A round cutter cannot mill a sharp inside corner. Two tools handle this; you enter the radius in the form on the left. Tool Effect Round Click a corner: it is rounded with the radius. Ear Click a corner: an arc is added around the corner point (dog-bone) so the cutter reaches fully into the corner and a square counterpart fits in. Changing the radius later At every rounded corner a label shows the radius, for example R5 . Long-press opens it for typing – like any dimension field with formulas and variables (it then shows R ƒfraeser/2 , for example). Clear the field and press Enter and the rounding disappears again.","n":false},{"t":"Pattern (array)","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/vervielfaeltigen/index.html","x":"Turn one shape into a rectangular pattern of linked copies that stays alive with the shape. The Pattern tool (between Offset and the vector operations) turns one selected shape into a rectangular pattern . Unlike a plain copy, the pattern stays live: the copies (the “children”) hang on the source shape and follow it. Select a shape, open the tool and fill in the window: Field Meaning Axis 1 (X) · Quantity How many shapes side by side (the source counted in). Axis 1 (X) · Distance Centre-to-centre distance across the width. Axis 2 (Y) · Quantity How many above one another. Axis 2 (Y) · Distance Centre-to-centre distance over the height. What makes a pattern special The source gets a small pattern marker (four dots). A double-click on it reopens the window for changes. The children are drawn semi-transparent (60 %), as shadows of the source. The children are firmly tied to the source: they can only be selected together, not one by one. Move the source and the whole pattern moves. Delete the source and the children go too. A toolpath or any other tool always applies to the source and acts on every child. Children have no toolpaths of their own. Dissolving a pattern With Ungroup ( ⇧⌘G ) you dissolve the pattern: from then on each copy stands on its own, gets the source's toolpaths copied onto it and can be edited individually. Quantity and distances take numbers, variables and formulas. For example platte_b / 50 columns fill the board whatever its width, and when a variable changes the pattern rebuilds – all in the spirit of parametric work. Every step can be undone.","n":true},{"t":"Grouping","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/gruppieren/index.html","x":"Combine several shapes into a group that selects like a single shape. Group ( ⌘G ) combines the selection: a click on any part then selects the whole group. Groups can themselves be grouped. Ungroup ( ⇧⌘G ) dissolves the outermost group of the selection, one level per click. Copies of a group form their own new group (kopieren-einfuegen).","n":false},{"t":"Import DXF","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/dxf-importieren/index.html","x":"Bring in drawings from other programs as a DXF file. The Import DXF tool reads a DXF file (R12, R14 and newer) and places its contents as one group in the drawing (gruppieren). Lines, arcs, circles and polylines become vectors you edit and give toolpaths like ones you drew yourself. If the file changes later in the other program, a right-click on the shape reloads it from disk.","n":false},{"t":"Image as a template","c":"Design tools","u":"Help/page/bild-als-vorlage/index.html","x":"Place a photo or sketch under the drawing, bring it to the right scale and trace it. Insert image loads an image (PNG, JPEG) onto an image layer (ebene-neu). It sits translucent under the drawing; set the opacity in the layer list. Calibrating To make the image to scale, click two points in the image with Calibrate image whose real distance you know (such as the ends of a ruler in the photo), and type the distance. CncCraft scales the image so it matches the board 1:1 – after that you can trace on it to exact size.","n":true},{"t":"Parametric design – overview","c":"Parametric design","u":"Help/page/parametrisch-uebersicht/index.html","x":"Parametric CAD for beginners: dimensions need not be fixed – you name, compute and connect them, and the drawing follows. The original Spark has neither variables nor constraints. In CncCraft a changed variable changes the drawing – all the way to the G-code, because the toolpaths recompute too. Working parametrically means you record what a dimension should mean (“10 mm from the edge”, “half the board's width”) instead of just drawing it once. Change a value later and CncCraft recomputes everything. There are five building blocks that combine freely – none needs prior knowledge from professional CAD programs: Building block What for Variables Named values used in formulas, for example rand = 12 . Formulas in dimension fields Every dimension field and depth does maths: platte_b - 2*rand . Dimension distances The distance between two points, a point and a line, or to the board edge stays as it is. Level/upright A line stays level or upright. Construction lines Lines from the ruler that shapes snap to and hang on: move the line – or change its formula – and the shapes move along. An example: you draw a sign and set rand . The outline gets the width platte_b - 2*rand and the height platte_l - 2*rand . Change the board size in the job later, or the value of rand , and the outline adapts, and the toolpaths follow. Limits CncCraft solves the constraints directly, without a general equation system: a shape that holds a constraint springs back when dragged. Moving with the mouse, node editing and boolean operations create new geometry without bindings; the status line and the behaviour point this out. Every step can be undone – trying things out costs nothing.","n":true},{"t":"Variables","c":"Parametric design","u":"Help/page/variablen/index.html","x":"Variables are named numbers or formulas you can use in every dimension field. The f(x) button next to “Set up job” in the toolbar opens the variables dialog. Your own variables With New variable you add a row. Every variable has: Column Meaning Name The name you use the variable by. It must not be empty, must not start with an underscore and must not occur twice. Expression A number or formula, also using other variables ( breite/2 - rand ). Value The computed result – or the error message if the formula does not work out. Comment A note for yourself. Change an expression and all dependent variables, shapes and toolpaths recompute. Variables may refer to other variables; a circular reference ( a = b + 1 , b = a ) is reported as an error instead of looping forever. If a formula uses a name that does not exist, CncCraft reports “Unknown variable”. From the job The job always provides three values: Name Meaning platte_b Board width (X) platte_l Board length (Y) platte_d Board thickness (Z) These names are taken; your own variables must not use them. Using variables Type the name into a dimension field, a constraint or a depth in a toolpath. As you type, a suggestion list of matching variables and their value appears under the field ( ↑ ↓ to choose, Tab or Enter to accept). More under formeln-in-feldern. Names with spaces or special characters go in curly braces: {Rand links} .","n":true},{"t":"Formulas in dimension fields","c":"Parametric design","u":"Help/page/formeln-in-feldern/index.html","x":"Every number field does the maths: arithmetic, brackets, powers and variables. In every dimension field – width, height, radius, position, constraint values and the start and cut depths of toolpaths – you can type a formula instead of a number. What is allowed Notation Meaning + - Add, subtract * × · Multiply (the letter x is not a times sign, so it can be a name) / ÷ Divide ^ Power ( 2^3 = 8; 2^3^2 is evaluated from the right) ( ) Brackets 12,5 or 12.5 Decimal (comma or point) breite , {Rand links} Variable (variablen) Multiplication before addition applies as usual. A = at the end evaluates the formula at once and replaces it with the number. Bound dimensions If a dimension field holds a formula with variables, the dimension is bound : change the variable and the shape adapts. In that case: The lower-left corner of the shape stays fixed; the top or right edge moves. The handles of a bound direction are locked (blue diamonds). Drag the free direction and only it changes. Change the value by hand in the form, or move nodes, and the binding is released. Typing a width or height on a rectangle or ellipse is technically the same as a distance constraint from the lower-left corner: the side is then fixed and drawn black ; free sides are blue . Errors If a formula is invalid or uses an unknown variable, the field turns red and names the reason (“Unknown variable: …”). While the field is invalid, Enter does not close it – Esc discards the entry. With the formula platte_d as the cut depth, a cut-out always cuts exactly through the material – even if you change the thickness in the job later.","n":true},{"t":"Dimension distances","c":"Parametric design","u":"Help/page/abstand-bemassen/index.html","x":"A distance constraint keeps two things a fixed distance apart – even when you move one or change variables. The Distance button in the “Constraints” group (key D ) turns on the dimensioning mode. How to do it Press D or click Distance . Pick the first object: a corner, the centre or a node of a shape, a line – or a corner or edge of the board . Pick the second object. Move the pointer to where the dimension should sit and click. Beside the points it measures level or upright, in between straight. Type the distance – a number, a formula or a variable – and press Enter . Esc takes back the last pick; another Esc ends the mode. What can be measured Point and point: level, upright or straight distance. Point and line: the perpendicular distance. A single line: its length – then click in the drawing to place the dimension. Two lines cannot be dimensioned (“Two lines cannot be dimensioned: pick a point”). Which one moves? The shape of the object picked first is the reference and stays put; the second shape moves until the distance is right. The board is always the reference. On one and the same shape only opposite corners are selectable (that is then width or height; the shape grows from the lower left). Editing and deleting dimensions Change the value: hold the value in the drawing (about half a second), then type. Move the dimension line: drag the dimension. The place is saved with it. Delete: right-click the value, or click it briefly and press Del . Bound dimensions appear blue, formulas as “ƒ formula = value mm”. The Show dimensions switch in the header shows or hides them; the mode always shows them. A shape that holds a dimension springs back to its place when dragged. Constraints are saved in the project and copied with the shape when all shapes involved are copied along. If a shape is deleted, its constraints fall away.","n":true},{"t":"Set level and upright","c":"Parametric design","u":"Help/page/waagrecht-senkrecht/index.html","x":"With one click a line stays level or upright – even when you drag the nodes. The Level/upright button in the “Constraints” group (key H ) turns on the mode. How to use it Click a line (a side of a shape or a segment of a polyline): CncCraft sets it level or upright, whichever the line is closer to. On a tie it becomes level. Or click two points one after another, from two different shapes as well. H or Esc ends the mode. What happens then On a polyline the second node gives way: it is set to the row or column of the first. Between two shapes the shape of the second point only moves sideways. Sides of rectangles and ellipses already lie that way; two points of the same shape that do not already lie so are rejected (“only works on the nodes of a vector”). The board is always the reference, never the second point. The constraint appears as a small blue symbol beside the line (red if it cannot be met). Click it and press Del – or right-click it – to delete it; the line then stays as it is.","n":true},{"t":"Construction lines on the ruler","c":"Parametric design","u":"Help/page/hilfslinien/index.html","x":"Red construction lines across the workpiece that shapes snap to and hang on – with a value, a formula and a lock. Spark has similar construction lines; in CncCraft they are also a soft constraint : shapes you drag onto a line stay attached and move along when the line moves – for example because a variable changes. Turning the rulers on and off The ruler button in the header (“Decoration” group) shows the rulers along the top and left edge of the board. Construction lines exist only while the rulers are on. Adding a construction line A click in the top ruler adds a vertical line there, a click in the left ruler a horizontal one. You can also press and drag in the ruler: the new line follows the mouse until you let go. Moving – only on the ruler Each line has a red handle in the ruler. Only there can it be dragged; over the handle the pointer shows a double arrow. In the drawing itself construction lines do not react – so they never get in the way of drawing. While dragging, the line always lands on whole millimetres . The exact distance Outside the ruler the line's distance from the left or bottom board edge is shown – in the same label as the values of distances. Over the number the pointer shows a hand: long-press opens it for typing. As in any dimension field, decimals, variables and formulas are allowed; a formula is kept (“ƒ platte_l/2 = 100 mm”), and the line follows when the variable or the board size changes. Drag a formula line by its handle and it becomes a fixed number again. Making shapes snap Drag a shape so close to a construction line that its left, right, top or bottom edge or its centre almost touches the line, and the shape turns red and the line grows thicker: let go now and the shape snaps exactly into place. The snapped shape hangs on the line : move the line, change its number or its formula, and the shape moves along – and with it the toolpaths. Drag the shape itself away again and it comes off the line. That is why it is a soft constraint. Snapping happens only when you drag a shape onto a line. Push a line across a resting shape and it does not attach. Selecting and deleting A click on the handle selects the line (it grows bolder), Del deletes it. The shapes that hung on it stay where they are. Locking construction lines In the corner where the rulers meet sits a lock . One click locks all construction lines; a short message confirms","n":true},{"t":"Toolpaths – overview","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/werkzeugwege-uebersicht/index.html","x":"A toolpath tells the router which shapes to machine and how. CncCraft has six kinds. On the right of the window, under “Board setup”, are six buttons for new toolpaths. The menu items under Toolpaths do the same. Toolpath What for Profile Runs along a contour – outside, inside or right on the line. Cut-out Cuts a part out of the material, with tabs. Pocket Clears the area inside a contour down to a depth. Slot Mills a slot the width of the cutter along a line – exactly as long as the line. Drilling Drills into the centre of circles and other contours. V-carving Carves lettering and drawings with a V-bit; the depth follows the width. How a toolpath is made Select the vectors to be machined. Click the button of the kind you want on the right. A form opens. Pick the tool and enter the depths (werkzeugauswahl). If a value exceeds what the material allows, a warning appears under the field. Click Apply . The toolpath appears in the list and as coloured lines in the drawing. Afterwards you manage the toolpaths in the list, check them in the preview and output them with Save. Hover over a strategy or cut direction and the tooltip shows a small drawing: where the cutter runs, which way it turns and what is cleared. Toolpaths remember which shapes they belong to. Change a shape or a variable (variablen) and CncCraft recomputes the toolpaths.","n":false},{"t":"The toolpath list","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/werkzeugwegliste/index.html","x":"The list shows all toolpaths with colour, kind, name and estimated milling time. Below the buttons for new toolpaths is the list. Element Meaning Handle on the left (⠿) Dragging changes the order – that is how the file is written later too. Eye Shows or hides the toolpath in the drawing, preview and output. Colour square and symbol The kind of toolpath and its colour in the drawing – the colour of its shapes' layer. Give the layer another colour and the entry and the cutter paths follow. Name A click selects the toolpath: its shapes are selected in the drawing, and it becomes the “selected” one for preview and export. Double-click opens the form to change it. Time The estimated milling time in minutes and seconds. Hide all / Show all switches all toolpaths at once. Tool entries: above the first toolpath and wherever a different tool begins, a small entry “Tool: …” appears – from there this cutter mills until a new entry comes. If two toolpaths in a row use the same tool, the entry appears only above the first. The ↻ button recomputes all toolpaths. Hover over a row and small buttons appear on the right: ✎ edit, 🗑 delete and ⏶ and ⏷ to move up and down. Below the list is the total milling time of the visible toolpaths. Hover over an entry and a tooltip names the kind, start and cut depth and the time. If a toolpath cannot be computed – for example because the tool is too large for the shape – the list shows a warning triangle with the reason.","n":true},{"t":"Tools and cutting values","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/werkzeugauswahl/index.html","x":"Every toolpath gets a tool with matching values for feed, speed and stepdown. In a toolpath's form you pick from a built-in list in the Tool field. It holds, per material, tools in several diameters with values that suit that material: End mills for profiles, cut-outs and pockets, Ball-nose cutters , V-bits with a tip angle for V-carving, Drills for holes. Which tools appear depends on the job's material (neuer-job). When a tool is chosen, the form takes its values; you can change each one. Field Meaning Start depth Where the cut begins, from the material surface (0 = surface). Cut depth How deep in total. For a cut-out the default is the material thickness. Stepdown How much deeper the cutter goes per pass. Depth and stepdown give the number of passes. Stepover The sideways distance between two passes for pockets and V-carving, in millimetres and as a percentage of the cutter diameter. Spindle speed Spindle revolutions per minute (rpm). Feed rate Cutting speed in mm/min. Plunge Speed when plunging into the material in mm/min. Tool no. The number that stands in the G-code file for the tool change. Start and cut depth take formulas and variables, for example platte_d/2 or platte_d (formeln-in-feldern). Warnings Every material has limits: the highest feed, the speed range and how deep the cutter may go per pass (for example two thirds of the diameter in hardwood). If a field exceeds a limit, a warning below it states what the material allows. Save anyway and CncCraft also writes the warning into the header of the G-code file. The shipped cutting values are starting points. Always check feed, speed and stepdown for your router and material, and mill a test in scrap material first.","n":false},{"t":"Profile toolpath","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/profil-werkzeugweg/index.html","x":"The profile runs the cutter along the contour – outside, inside or on the line. Select the vectors and click the Profile toolpath button ( Toolpaths ▸ Profile ). In addition to the general fields (werkzeugauswahl) you set: Cut Outside: the cutter runs outside the contour, offset by the cutter radius – for the part that stays. Inside: the cutter runs inside – for a hole or recess. On the line: the centre of the cutter runs exactly on the contour. Cut direction Climb: the cutter turns with the feed direction – usually the cleaner result. Conventional: against the feed direction. Tabs With Tabs , a few bridges are left along the contour to hold the part in the board until everything is milled. You set the number next to it (default: 4). The tabs form only in the lowest passes. Open lines are run back and forth, so the cutter does not fly back after every pass. If the cutter is too large for the shape, the list reports “Tool too large for this shape”.","n":false},{"t":"Cut-out toolpath","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/ausschnitt-werkzeugweg/index.html","x":"The cut-out is a profile that cuts a part all the way out and holds it with tabs. The cut-out ( Toolpaths ▸ Cut-out , also “cut part free”) is a profile toolpath with preset values for the most common case: freeing a part from the board. Setting Default for the cut-out Cut Outside Tabs on, 4 Cut depth the material thickness Tool End mill, 6 mm Every field can be changed in the form – they are the same as for the profile. After milling you cut the tabs with a knife or a saw and sand the spots. Enter platte_d as the cut depth and the cut-out always goes through the whole material, even if you change the thickness later.","n":false},{"t":"Pocket toolpath","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/taschen-werkzeugweg/index.html","x":"The pocket clears an area inside a closed contour down to a depth. Select closed vectors and click Pocket toolpath . Besides the general fields (werkzeugauswahl) you choose the clearing strategy : Strategy How the cutter clears Offset In rings that follow the contour, from the inside out. Between neighbouring rings the cutter stays down. Raster Back and forth in straight parallel passes, then one lap along the wall. The raster angle (in degrees) sets the direction. Spiral One continuous spiral from the inside out: no fresh start, each lap flows smoothly into the next. As with the profile there is the cut direction climb or conventional . The distance between neighbouring passes is the stepover ; depth and stepdown set the number of levels cleared one after another.","n":false},{"t":"Drilling toolpath","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/bohr-werkzeugweg/index.html","x":"The drilling toolpath drills into the centre of every selected contour. Select the shapes – usually small circles – and click Drilling toolpath . The drill moves to the centre of every closed contour and drills down to the cut depth . The stepdown here is the depth per peck: after each peck the drill lifts to clear the chips (“pecking”). The circle diameter does not set the hole – the chosen tool does. Draw the circles the size of the drill so the drawing is right. The defaults are a drill and 10 mm depth; change both in the form.","n":false},{"t":"V-carving toolpath","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/vcarving-werkzeugweg/index.html","x":"In V-carving a V-bit carves lettering and shapes; the wider the line, the deeper it goes. Select closed vectors – a piece of text, for example – and click V-carving toolpath . The tool is a V-bit; its tip angle and diameter determine the result. The depth at a point comes from its distance to the edge of the shape, divided by the tangent of half the tip angle. So in wide areas the cutter plunges deeper than in narrow ones. Field Meaning Cut depth The greatest depth the path may reach at all. Depth limit Limits how deep the tip goes in wide areas (in mm). 0 means: limited only by the cutter diameter. Stepover Distance between passes. Check V-carvings in the 3D preview (vorschau-werkzeugwege): there you see how deep the tips go and whether fine letters come out cleanly.","n":false},{"t":"Toolpath preview","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/vorschau-werkzeugwege/index.html","x":"The preview plays the toolpaths on the workpiece and shows what the router will remove. The Simulation & Export area at the bottom right has two buttons: Preview and Save . Preview starts the simulation; CncCraft switches to the 3D view for it. Control Effect ▶ Play Runs the toolpaths one after another; the material is removed as it goes. ⏭ At once Shows the end result straight away. ↺ Resets the preview to the untouched material. All / Selected / Visible Which toolpaths the preview shows: all, the one clicked in the list, or the visible ones. Speed How fast the animation runs. Detail How finely the material is drawn: “faster” on the left, “more precise” on the right. This only changes the display, not the toolpaths. Show G-code motion in 3D Overlays the toolpaths as coloured lines on the workpiece. The Detail slider (0–100 %) lets you choose between a fast rough view and a fine one. In the 3D view the view cube and the origin axis cross also help you keep your bearings. Milling time The list gives an estimated milling time per toolpath and, below it, the total time of the visible toolpaths (werkzeugwegliste). The estimate uses the set feeds and a flat rapid speed; the real time depends on your machine.","n":false},{"t":"Saving toolpaths","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/werkzeugwege-speichern/index.html","x":"For the router you save the toolpaths as a G-code file. Click Save in the Simulation & Export area at the bottom right – or choose File ▸ Save toolpaths … . What gets saved Selected toolpath: only the one clicked in the list. Visible toolpaths: all visible toolpaths in the order of the list. A line below states how many toolpaths are involved and – if several tools are needed one after another – how many tool changes that is. Several tools: one file or one per tool If the toolpaths need more than one tool, CncCraft asks when saving: One file per tool: for each run of toolpaths with the same tool a separate G-code file is made; all files go together into a ZIP. The files are named <name>_01_<tool>.nc , <name>_02_<tool>.nc and so on: first the general file name (the ZIP's name), then a running number in list order, then the tool (for example sign_01_End-mill-6-mm.nc ). So the files line up in the right milling order. Everything in one file: as before, one file with all toolpaths; before each tool change the change command stands ( T… M6 , not for GRBL). With the GRBL output, “everything in one file” is not possible with several tools: GRBL has no tool change, so only one file per tool remains. A tool change counts only where the tool changes from the toolpath before: two toolpaths in a row with the same cutter stay in one file. If the same cutter appears again later after a different one, that is a new section with its own file. Post-processor The Post-processor choice sets how the G-code is written: Entry Extension Unit G-code (mm) .nc Millimetres G-code (inch) .tap Inches GRBL (mm) .gcode Millimetres, for GRBL controllers Then click Save toolpaths … and choose the destination and file name. Check every new file in the preview and run it first without material or in scrap. Make sure the origin and safe height (neuer-job) match your machine.","n":true},{"t":"Slot toolpath","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/nut-werkzeugweg/index.html","x":"The slot mills a channel along a vector, as wide as the cutter. The slot toolpath does not exist in the Spark template. Select one or more vectors – open or closed – and click Slot . The slot is exactly as wide as the cutter; so there is no stepover. Setting Meaning Side On the line : the centre of the slot lies on the vector. Left / Right : the slot lies beside the vector, one edge on the line. Cap For open lines: Outer – the cutter runs past the end point, the slot becomes longer than the vector by the cutter radius. Inner – the cutter stops half a diameter before the end point, the slot is exactly as long as the vector. Cut direction Climb or conventional . Ramp angle The cutter plunges at an angle instead of straight down; 0° = straight. In the form, hover over an option: a picture in the tooltip shows what it does – for example where the cutter stops with Inner and Outer , or how the turning and feed relate with climb and conventional.","n":true},{"t":"Materials and limits","c":"Toolpaths","u":"Help/page/materialien/index.html","x":"Every workpiece is made of a material. It knows its limits, recommends cutters and cooling and sets the look in 3D. The Spark template knows materials only as names in the tool database. In CncCraft every material has limits and recommendations, and CncCraft warns when a toolpath exceeds them – for beginners the most important help against broken cutters. Choosing a material When you create a new job, the material is mandatory: only once one is chosen can the job be created (neuer-job). CncCraft suggests the one used last. Under the picker the Recommendations area unfolds the description, cutting values, suitable cutters and cooling. Shipped are softwood, hardwood, plywood, MDF, acrylic, HDPE/plastic, rigid foam, aluminium, brass and copper . What a material knows Value Meaning Max. feed The cutter should not move through the material faster (mm/min). Speed The recommended spindle range (rpm). Max. stepdown How deep per pass at most, as a fraction of the cutter diameter. A rule of thumb for wood: at most two thirds of the diameter. Cutter Which cutters suit: number of flutes, material, spiral direction. Cooling Whether and with what to cool – for example isopropanol for aluminium. Look How the workpiece looks in the 3D preview. Warnings In the toolpath form a note appears right under stepdown, feed, plunge or speed when the value is outside the recommendation – for example 6 mm stepdown with a 5 mm cutter in hardwood. In the header of the G-code file there is a section “WARNING: TOO FAST OR TOO DEEP FOR THE MATERIAL” with every affected toolpath (werkzeugwege-speichern). Slower and shallower than recommended is never a problem and is not reported. Look in the 3D preview The preview shows the workpiece in the look of its material: grain for wood, the layers at a plywood edge, brushed and shiny metal. Your own materials Manage … at the end of the material picker opens material management. With + you make a copy of the chosen material – with its tools, trimmed to the new material's limits. Name, description, limits, recommendations and look are editable. Shipped materials and the current job's material cannot be deleted. The limits are cautious starting values for hobby routers. Your machine and your cutters may do more or less – mill a test in scrap material first.","n":true},{"t":"Version 0.1.0 – feature set","c":"Versions","u":"Help/page/version-0-1-0/index.html","x":"What the current version can do, and what is new compared with the Spark template. CncCraft is free: no licence, no subscription, no account. It is aimed at beginners who want to design parametrically without having to learn a big CAD program. What CncCraft 0.1.0 can do Jobs with size, material and origin; save and open projects and reopen recent ones (neuer-job, dateien-speichern). Drawing: line, spline, rectangle, circle, ellipse, polygon, text (auswahlwerkzeug); import DXF, images as a template. Editing: nodes, offset, pattern (array), mirror, centre, convert to curves, boolean operations, grouping, trim, delete and join, rounding and ears, copy and paste (knotenbearbeitung, boolesche-operationen). Layers with visibility, lock and colour (ebenenliste). Toolpaths: profile, cut-out, pocket (offset, raster, spiral), slot, drilling, V-carving (werkzeugwege-uebersicht). Preview in 2D and 3D and output as G-code (mm, inch, GRBL) (vorschau-werkzeugwege, werkzeugwege-speichern). Light and dark theme (einstellungen-design). New compared with the Spark template Variables and formulas in every dimension field and depth. Distance constraints and level/upright solved directly. Copy and paste including toolpaths and constraints. View cube and origin axis cross in 3D; worksheet decorations you switch on one by one. Single keys for tools (S, L, P, R, C, O, T, D, H). The preview's “Detail” slider. Tool entries in the toolpath list and, when saving, the choice between one file or one file per tool in a ZIP. A warning about unsaved changes on closing and on replacing the drawing. Construction lines from the ruler: shapes snap and hang on them, distances take formulas and variables, whole millimetres while dragging, a lock against accidental moves. A material library with ten materials, your own materials, recommendations, warnings for cuts that are too fast or too deep, and material surfaces in the 3D view. The slot toolpath and the spiral strategy for pockets. Undo for every action on layers, toolpaths and construction lines. One shared input field for all values in the drawing (dimensions, distances, radii, construction lines): the same suggestions, formulas and error messages everywhere.","n":false}];